{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model underestimates the level by ~+1.0 to +2.5 EUR for most of the horizon when the market is in an upswing (examples 1–3). Apply an upward level shift, strongest in days 1–15.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: after an upward regime shift, the model follows but with insufficient slope (it reaches the new level too slowly). Add a positive trend correction proportional to recent 5–10d momentum.",
    "Late-horizon downside overshoot: when the model turns bearish, it often drops too far versus realized prices (examples 4–5, last ~7–10 days). Damp negative moves beyond ~day 20 by shrinking deviations toward the recent mean.",
    "Overreaction to short-term reversals: model sometimes predicts sharp pullbacks after highs that do not materialize (examples 4–5). Cap forecasted drawdowns to a fraction (e.g., 50–70%) of the model-implied drop unless confirmed by multi-day negative momentum.",
    "Horizon-specific calibration: use stronger upward bias correction early (days 1–15), and stronger mean-reversion/damping late (days 20–30) to avoid exaggerated end-of-horizon swings."
  ]
}
